{
  "reply": "According to Arlen Moss’s April 2020 cultivation note from Bracken Pool nursery, the invented marsh plant is greyreed. The wrapping material was damp paper. The note says eighteen adult-collected greyreed seeds received a twelve-minute exposure inside damp paper, and that the paper wraps were removed before planting; the wraps were not buried with the seeds. The note does not give further procedural detail about how the paper was folded, held, or physically removed beyond that removal-before-planting point.

For the tray setup, there were two neighboring trays filled from the same soil batch. One condition had eighteen seeds prepared in damp paper; the other condition had eighteen untreated seeds. Both trays received the same watering schedule. Tray positions were exchanged each afternoon so that one treatment would not remain continuously nearest the door.

The emergence results recorded first visible emergence by the seventh morning: twelve of the eighteen prepared seeds had visible shoots, compared with nine of the eighteen untreated seeds. The tally counts first visible emergence only, not survival to maturity. A blank observation cell means the seed position was not assessed that morning, not that it failed to emerge, and unseen seeds were not replaced.

The limited follow-up involved selecting five emerged plants from the prepared tray and keeping them under observation for another six days. All five still had visible shoots at the last inspection. However, those five were chosen because their shoots were easy to locate, so the follow-up was not a random sample of all emerged plants and could not estimate survival for the full planting. The note restricts its conclusion to these two trays and the recorded observation interval, without establishing a mechanism or broader performance across other soils, seasons, or nurseries."
}

{
  "reply": "The cultivation note says: (1) it is the Larch Mere nursery’s May 2022 report, and the invented marsh plant is amberrush. (2) The wrapping material was damp paper. The procedure was that one group of seeds spent fifteen minutes in damp paper before sowing, and the paper was removed before the seeds entered the soil; the report does not describe any buried wrapping or continuing paper cover. (3) The setup used two trays of amberrush seeds collected from adult plants, with twenty seeds assigned to each tray. One tray received the damp-paper treatment and the other was planted without it. Both trays used material from one soil batch, were watered on the same schedule, and had their locations swapped daily so neither stayed next to the doorway. (4) By the eighth morning, fourteen positions in the prepared tray showed shoots, compared with ten positions in the untreated tray. These counts describe emergence only, not mature plants; nonemerging seeds were left in place, and a blank sheet entry was not recorded as a failure to emerge. (5) The limited follow-up observed six easily located shoots for five additional days. All six remained visible, but the note cautions that this selection favored convenient plants and cannot establish a survival rate for the whole sowing. Jana Pell treats it as a limited check, not a test of later flowering."
}

